{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 8 onward. Apply a downward level shift (increasing with horizon).",
    "Horizon-dependent overestimation: small errors in the first ~1–5 days, but errors widen materially in days ~10–30. Use a correction that ramps down with lead time (e.g., larger haircut after day 7–10).",
    "Underestimates downside momentum: during selloffs, the model mean-reverts too quickly and fails to follow the continued decline. Add a trend/momentum term that keeps forecasts lower when the last 1–2 weeks show a clear downtrend.",
    "Overshoot after short-term rebounds: when history shows a bounce, the model projects an extended rally (often into mid/high 70s) that does not materialize. Cap rebound strength and enforce stronger mean reversion after 3–7 days.",
    "Volatility/peak inflation: forecast paths exhibit higher amplitude than realized (too-high peaks and insufficiently low troughs). Damp forecast deviations from the recent mean (shrink toward recent average), with stronger damping at longer horizons.",
    "Late-horizon drift up: beyond ~day 15, forecasts tend to drift upward even when the realized path stays flat-to-down. Impose a non-positive drift constraint when the recent 10–20 day slope is negative."
  ]
}
